    "knowledge_point": "Sketching the Solution Curves of a Differential Equation",
    "problem": "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4{e}^{2x}?$",
    "worked_solution": "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-4{e}^{2x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-4\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-2,0,2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011667/assets/cc2ee25a85be3ffdb055853f7665bd53d34003a74b96a14bb947bed39b18e83f.png)",
